# ===========================================================================
# data/ — deny-by-default for tabular data files
# ===========================================================================
# This is a second, local copy of the rule in the repository root .gitignore,
# deliberately duplicated. This directory is the one place in the project where
# licensed third-party corpus text lands, and the cost of a mistake here is a
# licence breach in a public repository. If someone edits or reorders the root
# file, this one still holds.
#
# Two of the three source corpora cannot be redistributed at all:
#   - Quora Question Pairs — Quora Terms of Service, no redistribution grant
#   - SODD                 — CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
# Every .csv and .tsv here is therefore ignored unless it is named below.
#
# Patterns in this file are relative to data/.
#
# To add an exception you must be able to state why the file is publishable:
# either it contains no third-party corpus text at all, or its text is
# synthetic and carries no upstream licence. If you cannot state that, it does
# not go on the list.

*.csv
*.tsv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Update 2026-08-04 — the rule above covered only .csv/.tsv. That was a gap.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `levy.dataset.io.save_dataset()` writes the working dataset in BOTH formats:
# ground_truth.full.csv *and* ground_truth.full.json. Only the .csv side was
# deny-by-default; the .json side was protected by its exact filename alone,
# so `data/annotated-900.json`, `data/backup.json` or any other JSON export of
# the same 900 pairs was NOT ignored and would have been staged by `git add .`.
# The audit found exactly that shape of file (annotation_progress.backup.json)
# untracked and unignored, next to an annotated-900.backup.csv that the .csv
# rule did catch.
#
# The same argument applies to the raw corpus formats: a shard copied or
# sampled OUT of raw/ into data/ (SODD parquet, PIT-2015 .data) was covered
# only while it stayed under raw/.
#
# So the inversion is now extended to every data-bearing extension in this
# directory. Same admission rule as above: to add an exception you must be
# able to state that the file carries no third-party corpus text at all, or
# that its text is synthetic and carries no upstream licence.
*.json
*.parquet
*.parquet.gzip
*.data

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ allowed
# 15 synthetic fixture pairs. Fabricated text, source_corpus =
# "synthetic-fixture". No third-party licence. The offline test suite and the
# scripts/reproduce.sh defaults read this file.
!ground_truth.csv

# The published D2 artifact: identifiers and labels only. Carries pair_id,
# workload, source_corpus, source_pair_id, original_label, author_label and
# metadata — and no query text in any column, because DistributionRecord has no
# text fields. This is what a third party rehydrates from.
!ground_truth.ids.csv

# Added 2026-08-04 alongside the *.json rule above. Each is admissible for a
# stated reason, not because it was already tracked:
#
#   ground_truth.json         the JSON twin of the 15 synthetic fixture pairs;
#                             fabricated text, source_corpus =
#                             "synthetic-fixture", no third-party licence.
#   corpora.json              provenance registry — URLs, snapshots, licences,
#                             filenames, checksums, citations. No query text.
#   ground_truth.ids.meta.json  the sampling run manifest: seed, ratio, adapter
#                             options, corpus snapshots, input checksums, tool
#                             versions. No query text, by construction.
!ground_truth.json
!corpora.json
!ground_truth.ids.meta.json

# ------------------------------------------------- deliberately NOT allowed
# Named here so the reasoning is on the record, not to add a rule — the
# blanket *.csv above already covers them:
#
#   ground_truth.full.csv        the rehydrated working dataset — full query
#                                text from all three corpora. Regenerate it
#                                with scripts/rehydrate_dataset.py; never
#                                commit it.
#   annotated-900.backup.csv     any hand-made backup or export of the working
#   *.backup.csv, *export*.csv   dataset carries exactly the same text.
#
# Anything under raw/ is already excluded by the root .gitignore
# (`data/raw/**`), which keeps the per-corpus directories but never their
# contents. See raw/README.md.
#
# Update 2026-08-04 — also deliberately NOT allowed, now covered by *.json:
#
#   ground_truth.full.json       the JSON half of the rehydrated working
#                                dataset. Same corpus text as the .csv half.
#   annotated-900.backup.json    the JSON shape of a hand-made backup. Until
#   *.backup.json, *export*.json 2026-08-04 these were not ignored at all.
#   annotation_progress.json     the annotation working file. It carries pair
#                                ids and the author's labels only — no corpus
#                                text, so this is a procedure rule, not a
#                                licence rule (docs/DATA_PRODUCTION.md step 9:
#                                "superseded by the labels in the ids file").
#                                It is deliberately absent from the allow-list
#                                above. NOTE: it was committed in e4aaa6f
#                                before this rule existed, and .gitignore does
#                                not untrack an already-tracked file — clearing
#                                that needs `git rm --cached`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What these rules do NOT protect against
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitignore is a staging filter. It is bypassed by:
#
#   git add -f <path>       explicit override
#   git stash --all         stashes IGNORED files too, into refs/stash
#   git stash -u            stashes untracked files
#
# The last two are not hypothetical: a `git stash --all` on 2026-08-04 wrote
# data/annotated-900.backup.csv — 900 rows of Quora QQP + SODD + PIT-2015 query
# text — into this repository's object database, where `*.csv` could not reach
# it. Only refs/stash pointed at it and nothing was pushed, but a
# `git push --mirror` pushes refs/stash, and a filesystem copy of .git carries
# it regardless.
#
# `scripts/audit_release.sh` check 4 is the backstop that catches this class of
# mistake, because it scans `git log --all` — which includes refs/stash — not
# just the working tree. Run it before any release or any push of a new ref.
